description: >-
  Starlink Performance (Gen 3) enterprise satellite terminal. Electronically
  steered phased-array dish with a rack-mountable Advanced Power Supply
  (AC/DC input, high-power PoE to the dish, LAN port out). IP69K, -40 to 60 C,
  in-motion capable. Router not included; pairs with Starlink Gen 3 Router,
  Router Mini, or a third-party router/firewall.
comments: |
  This device type consolidates the two physical units shipped in the
  Performance (Gen 3) Kit: the Performance Dish and the Advanced Power Supply.
  The dish itself has no NetBox-mappable ports (proprietary push-fit Performance
  connector). All patchable ports live on the Advanced Power Supply and are
  modeled below.

  ANTENNA (Starlink Performance Dish)
  Type: electronically steered phased array
  Field of view: 140 degrees
  Orientation: software-assisted manual orienting
  Dimensions: 609 x 396 x 40 mm (24 x 15.6 x 1.6 in)
  Weight: 5.2 kg (11.5 lb)
  Environmental: IP69K (cables installed), IP68 (no cables)
  Operating temp: -40 to 60 C (-40 to 140 F)
  Wind: survivable 280 kph+ (174 mph+)
  Snow melt: up to 85 mm/hour (3.5 in/hour)
  Power consumption: 75-100 W average
  Throughput: up to 400+ Mbps (gigabit service plans planned for 2026, no
  hardware change)
  Design life: 10-year mission life

  ADVANCED POWER SUPPLY
  Dimensions: 310 x 180 x 40 mm (12.2 x 7.1 x 1.6 in)
  Weight: 2.1 kg (4.6 lb)
  Environmental: IP68 (cables installed/plugged)
  Operating temp: -40 to 60 C
  Power input: 100-240 VAC, 12-56 VDC (DC input supports backup battery)
  Features: rack-mountable, smart diagnostics, high-power PoE port, LAN port

  KIT CONTENTS
  Performance Dish, Advanced Power Supply, 25 m (82 ft) Performance cable,
  1.5 m AC power cable, 1.5 m DC power cable, 5 m (16.4 ft) Ethernet cable.
  Performance cable options: 5 m, 25 m (included), 50 m, 50 m LSZH.

  NOTES
  LAN port speed is not published on the datasheet; modeled as 1000base-t
  (gigabit) as the sensible default. Adjust to 2.5gbase-t if a faster port is
  confirmed on your unit. The dish-facing port uses Starlink's proprietary
  high-power PoE (not IEEE 802.3), modeled as an "other" interface, PSE.
  Source: Starlink Performance Specifications sheet (specification_sheet_performance.pdf),
  retrieved 2026-08-17.
